/**
* @page busphysics Bus-Physics
* 
* # 1-Wire Timing
*
* ## Datasheet Values
*
* All timing parameters have been selected in accordance with Maxim (Dallas)
* data sheets. According to the DS2438 data sheet, the following limits are
* specified for timing parameters.
* 
* | Parameter            | Symbol           | MIN   | MAX   | Units |
* | :---                 |                  | :---: | :---: | :---: |
* | Time Slot            | t<sub>SLOT</sub> | 60    | 120   | µs    |
* | Recovery Time        | t<sub>REC</sub>  | 1     |       | µs    |
* | Write 0 Low Time     | t<sub>LOW0</sub> | 60    | 120   | µs    |
* | Write 1 Low Time     | t<sub>LOW1</sub> | 1     | 15    | µs    |
* | Read Data Valid      | t<sub>RDV</sub>  |       | 15    | µs    |
* | Reset Time High      | t<sub>RSTH</sub> | 480   |       | µs    |
* | Reset Time Low       | t<sub>RSTL</sub> | 480   | 980   | µs    |
* | Presence Detect High | t<sub>PDH</sub>  | 15    | 60    | µs    |
* | Presence Detect Low  | t<sub>PDL</sub>  | 60    | 240   | µs    |
* 
*
* ## Adjusted values ​​for this library
* 
* The timing parameters used are defined in a `constexpr` block and have been
* adjusted to remain within the specified tolerances.
* (@ref owtimingConstants "1-Wire Timing constants")
* 
* - TIME_RECOVERY - This period allows devices to recover after a write or read
* operation. It is intentionally not used because the bus participants are
* powered by an external voltage supply.
* - TIME_RESET_LOW - This is the time for a reset pulse from the master; it
* has been set to 500 µs to ensure a reliable reset is performed.
* - TIME_PRESENCE_HIGH - During this time, the bus must go HIGH before the
* participants pull it LOW again to signal their presence. (20µs)
* - TIME_PRESENCE_LOW - Once the time for the HIGH presence pulse has elapsed,
* the system waits for the LOW presence pulse. (70µs)
* - TIME_RESET_HIGH - This duration must be observed until the complete reset
* cycle has finished. The timing starts from the transition of the reset signal
* from the low state to the high state. This specific duration is calculated
* separately because, otherwise, it would be skewed by the TIME_PRESENCE_LOW
* and TIME_PRESENCE_HIGH intervals.
*
* @todo Send a reset, then poll the bus for a High state; if High is detected
* within the time limit, proceed to the next step. If no High signal appears,
* monitor the maximum duration using microsecond ticks and abort with an error.
* 
* ## Simple Hardware Interface on MCUs
*
* The simplest way to operate a OneWire bus on a microcontroller requires only
* one pin. This pin handles both bus control and reading data sent to the
* master.This is achieved by using the "Data Direction Registers" to enable a
* rapid sequence of pull-down and subsequent read slots. The pin is continuously
* set to low. When the master wants to pull the bus low, the pin is configured
* as an output via the DDR. Then, the pin is switched to an input, and the read
* cycle can begin immediately. This requires an external pull-up resistor on the
* pin to pull the bus high again. The advantage is an extremely simple hardware
* interface without additional components. The disadvantage is that the pull-up
* resistor limits the network size, as larger networks require a strong pull-up
* resistor. Techniques like slew rate control cannot be implemented with this
* method, and parasitic mode is also problematic.
*
* The pin's wiring:
*
* @code
* ┌──────────┐     +5V
* ┆          │      │
* ┆          │     ┌┴┐
* ┆          │     │ │ 10k
* ┆          │     └┬┘             Bus
* ┆  MCU PIN ├──────┴────<>────┬────────┬────>
* ┆          │              ┌──┴──┐  ┌──┴──┐
* ┆          │              │ Dev.│  │ Dev.│
* ┆          │              │  1  │  │  2  │
* ┆          │              └──┬──┘  └──┬──┘
* ┆          │                 │        │   
* └──────────┘                GND      GND  
* @endcode
*/
